cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use marola, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
title: "marola: an open, non-profit platform for sea conditions and bathing-water quality at Brazilian beaches, built on public data"
abstract: >-
  marola tells you whether and when to go into the water at a beach, and why. It is a map of the
  beaches of Florianópolis, Rio de Janeiro and Salvador, each scored 0-100 for today and tomorrow
  from the sea, wind, waves, tide and the official bathing-water quality. Every number comes from
  public data: OpenStreetMap for the beaches, Open-Meteo for the sea and weather forecast, and the
  bulletins of IMA/SC, INEA (RJ) and INEMA (BA) for water quality. Anything that could put a
  swimmer at risk is decided by plain rules anyone can read. A language model only writes a short
  summary, a second automatic pass checks it against the numbers, and it never changes a score or
  drops an alert. When a source does not answer, marola says "no data".

  marola starts from water quality because basic sanitation in Brazil is far from solved: before
  surfing, fishing or diving comes the question of whether the water is fit to enter. It is open
  source and non-profit because much of Brazil's ocean data is held privately, and the project's
  promise is to give everything it gathers back to everyone.

  This record archives the marola umbrella: the design proposals (MIPs), the ways of working, the
  aggregated documentation and the pinned versions of every repository. The project is a
  distributed monolith, one plan with each part in its own repository: marola-app (the pipeline
  that joins forecast, beaches and water quality and computes the score), marola-site (the map at
  marola.dev), marola-oods (the Open Ocean Data Store, an open archive of beach and water
  history), marola-corpus (sourced notes about the sea), marola-ml (where models are trained and
  tested before use) and marola-devkit (the shared development tools).
